</node>
<node id="&quot;CONTROL&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Control denotes the ability to manage or govern, which is questioned by an entity that creates its own laws, challenging the established order."&lt;SEP&gt;"Control here refers to the ability to govern or direct football policy, which Ratcliffe must navigate with Glazers' influence."&lt;SEP&gt;"Control is an Amulet item available to Sorcerers, typically used across various builds, providing buffs that enhance existing abilities like Fireball and potentially offering crowd control effects."&lt;SEP&gt;"Control in this context refers to governance and authority within the company, which is challenged by the actions and changes introduced by Edward Rogers and the dynamics following the deaths of key figures like Loretta Rogers, Alan Horn, and Phil Lind."&lt;SEP&gt;"Control, in this context, relates to the challenge posed by the unknown intelligence that writes its own rules, contrasting with human attempts to manage or understand it.&lt;SEP&gt;"Control in video games often refers to the player's ability to manage and regulate in-game actions or enemies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Control here refers to the regulation of CO2 emissions and the balance between energy production and carbon capture technologies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Control is a concept referring to the governance, strategy, and performance management within the context of AFL, challenged by high-level player capabilities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Control, as used in the context of fighters and strategies, highlights a key aspect of combat sports, wherein dominance and the ability to dictate the pace of the fight are crucial."&lt;SEP&gt;"Control, in the text, is depicted as a concept in governance and power, against which other entities or ideas are resisting or challenging."&lt;SEP&gt;"Control, in the context of robotics, refers to the ability to manage or govern systems, which becomes complex when dealing with intelligent entities that can independently define their rules."&lt;SEP&gt;"Control in boxing refers to the strategic management of the fight, which is challenged when facing an intelligence (or a highly skilled opponent like Benavidez)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Control in this context relates to the offside rule in hockey, which is being discussed in terms of its challenge by the opposing team's offensive strategy."&lt;SEP&gt;"Control refers to the ability or authority to manage or govern, which is challenged by the intelligence entity that writes its own rules."&lt;SEP&gt;"Control is the ability to manage or govern, here challenged by an advanced intelligence that operates on its own rules."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0309800f9c50f78faa6f2eefdb586390&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be445145c5fb78ff5ed8900127ea4180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e3d44e247d9d2e2429746d7c3565834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aace41eac2a185a9a50fb79e20561264&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b7d18ee3e795fba2189434b856857270&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63cd65182fe27e88c442d035f5b1524e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ccb78a9662e9f89c12554eea5d98b637&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718735513dd5c30904ba5902e9518734&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b0301adc7639c257e028097975e7770&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fe8a061c762ff2ea1635b13e952f2ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73262bffb070c1a74f8727465de445e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc33b11c05922b5754d12e7ba46b10b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcee3fd9a0053b638a37cd7b4ed221b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e6b96f31b61f54eab9da738fca5e668&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90a6a6b310c78870861a1b31314bb9c5</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INTELLIGENCE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Intelligence here refers to an advanced, rule-writing entity that communicates, challenging traditional notions of control."&lt;SEP&gt;"Intelligence in the text refers to an entity or group that writes its own rules, indicating autonomy and the challenge it poses to established control mechanisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Intelligence is described as an entity capable of self-rule, redefining communication, and altering human contact paradigms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Intelligence is discussed in the context of technological and human sources, with the implication that human sources provide interpretation and decision-making context that technology alone does not offer."&lt;SEP&gt;"Intelligence, an unknown entity capable of self-communication and altering rules, is central to the team's mission and the challenges they face."&lt;SEP&gt;"Intelligence, characterized by its ability to self-learn and communicate, is at the heart of the team's interaction and holds significant implications for human history.&lt;SEP&gt;"Intelligence refers to the unknown entity that is sending communications, a central concept in the discussion about first contact and the challenges it presents."&lt;SEP&gt;"Intelligence refers metaphorically to the opposing team's strategic thinking and adaptability on the ice, likened to an entity capable of learning and communication."&lt;SEP&gt;"Intelligence refers to an advanced entity capable of self-rule and communication with the team."</data>
