<node id="&quot;MXENES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"MXenes are a class of materials known for their ability to absorb carbon dioxide, and are being researched for their potential in energy storage and environmental applications."&lt;SEP&gt;"MXenes are a class of tiny materials with a nanoscale structure that can capture carbon dioxide (CO2) from the air, potentially helping to solve the problem of climate change."&lt;SEP&gt;"MXenes are materials being considered for carbon capture due to their ability to absorb CO2, with concerns about their production carbon footprint."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-04ef2f90e39fb40c4d821653a7704080&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63cd65182fe27e88c442d035f5b1524e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-563db99445902ad4de5c6a8a7e52a02b</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MAX PHASES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Max phases are lab-made compounds that can be converted into MXenes by removing extra metal layers with strong acids, offering tunable properties for various applications."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-04ef2f90e39fb40c4d821653a7704080</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;VANADIUM&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ELEMENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Vanadium is a transition metal used in the structure of some MXene layers."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-04ef2f90e39fb40c4d821653a7704080</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CARBON&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ELEMENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Carbon is used in the structure of some MXene layers, contributing to their ability to capture CO2."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-04ef2f90e39fb40c4d821653a7704080</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LINKÖPING UNIVERSITY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Linköping University is where materials scientist Per Persson works, contributing research on MXenes."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-04ef2f90e39fb40c4d821653a7704080</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MICHAEL NAGUIB&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Michael Naguib is an engineering physicist at Tulane University who contributed to the development of MXenes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Michael Naguib is involved in research on MXenes, contributing to the field's understanding of their properties and applications."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-04ef2f90e39fb40c4d821653a7704080</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TULANE UNIVERSITY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Tulane University is an educational institution located in New Orleans, La., noted for making the world’s first MXene in 2011, a titanium carbide."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tulane University is where Michael Naguib works, contributing to MXene research and development."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-04ef2f90e39fb40c4d821653a7704080&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-563db99445902ad4de5c6a8a7e52a02b</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;STEPHEN COOK&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Stephen Cook is mentioned as one of the creators of MXenes, with his work acknowledged building on the idea of titanium carbide MXene."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-04ef2f90e39fb40c4d821653a7704080</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;COAL-FIRED POWER PLANT IN ARIZONA&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Coal-fired power plant in Arizona is cited as a place where MXene materials could potentially be installed to capture CO2 from emissions."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-04ef2f90e39fb40c4d821653a7704080</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;EXHAUST&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Exhaust refers to the byproducts, including unseen CO2 gases, released from coal-fired power plant stacks."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-04ef2f90e39fb40c4d821653a7704080</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;STEAM AND CARBON DIOXIDE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Steam and carbon dioxide refer to the exhaust gases from power plants, with CO2 being the target of capture by MXene materials."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-04ef2f90e39fb40c4d821653a7704080</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;GRAPHENE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Graphene is mentioned as another example of a two-dimensional material, contrasting with MXenes, indicating their structural similarities.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-04ef2f90e39fb40c4d821653a7704080</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SODIUM HYDROXIDE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Sodium hydroxide is noted as a milder substance used in the process to create MXenes, illustrating a safer method of production."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-04ef2f90e39fb40c4d821653a7704080</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MOLTEN FLUORIDE SALTS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Molten fluoride salts are used as an alternative method to create MXenes, avoiding harsh acids and still achieving the desired reaction at high temperatures."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-04ef2f90e39fb40c4d821653a7704080</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CO2&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"CO2 represents the greenhouse gases targeted for capture and reduction to mitigate climate change."&lt;SEP&gt;"CO2 stands for carbon dioxide, the greenhouse gas targeted for capture by MXene materials to mitigate climate change."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-04ef2f90e39fb40c4d821653a7704080&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63cd65182fe27e88c442d035f5b1524e</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;POWER PLANTS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Power plants are significant sources of CO2 emissions and potential application sites for MXene-based carbon capture technologies."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-04ef2f90e39fb40c4d821653a7704080</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ATMOSPHERIC CO2&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Atmospheric CO2 refers to the carbon dioxide present in Earth's atmosphere, which is a major focus for capture to reduce the impact of climate change."</data>
