  <data key="d2">chunk-cd8d316ba20246a652c2799173f4e183</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HOLIDAY TIMING&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Holiday Timing indicates the strategic timing of the product refresh announcements in relation to the holiday season, potentially to drive consumer sales and interest."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-cd8d316ba20246a652c2799173f4e183</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MELISSA LUCASHENKO&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Melissa Lucashenko is a contemporary novelist who has won the Miles Franklin Literary Award and is known for using Aboriginal language in her work, depicting modern Aboriginal life with urgency and humor."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e6a588ff07fea97f8a257510a280d98f</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TOM PETRIE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Tom Petrie is an historical figure, a pioneer of Queensland and a significant character in Melissa Lucashenko's novel, Too Much Lip, who navigated early colonial life and had interactions with Aboriginal people."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e6a588ff07fea97f8a257510a280d98f</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;REMINISCENCES OF EARLY QUEENSLAND&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Reminiscences of Early Queensland is a historical text that sparked Lucashenko's interest and influenced her writing, particularly her novel Too Much Lip, which explores colonial Brisbane and Aboriginal experiences."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e6a588ff07fea97f8a257510a280d98f</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TOO MUCH LIP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Too Much Lip is Melissa Lucashenko's novel that won the Miles Franklin Literary Award, highlighting narratives of defiance and survival during colonial times."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e6a588ff07fea97f8a257510a280d98f</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;QUEENSLAND PENAL COLONY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Queensland penal colony refers to the historical setting where the pioneering life of Thomas Petrie and the early interactions between Aboriginal people and settlers took place, now Brisbane, as depicted in Lucashenko's works."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e6a588ff07fea97f8a257510a280d98f</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SALTWATER COUNTRY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Saltwater country is a region in southern Queensland mentioned in the novel Too Much Lip, where the protagonist Mulanyin originates from and where he envisages a traditional lifestyle for himself and Nita."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e6a588ff07fea97f8a257510a280d98f</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;THOMAS PETRIE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"HISTORICAL FIGURE"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Thomas Petrie is a figure who young Mulanyin works with, indicating a mentorship or professional relationship."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thomas Petrie was a significant pioneer of Queensland, mentioned as an influential character and the subject of Lucashenko's novel Too Much Lip, depicting his life during the colonial era."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71e7bc3cc42173f46acd57affd5c8aee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6a588ff07fea97f8a257510a280d98f</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;YUGAMBEH COUNTRY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Yugambeh country represents the traditional homeland that the protagonist Mulanyin wishes to take Nita to, underscoring the connection to Aboriginal heritage and the conflict with colonial systems."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e6a588ff07fea97f8a257510a280d98f</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MULANYIN&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Mulanyin is a character who falls in love with Nita, linking her to a personal and possibly cultural narrative."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71e7bc3cc42173f46acd57affd5c8aee</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;NITA&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Nita has lived with the Petries since childhood, suggesting a long-term familial or servant relationship."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71e7bc3cc42173f46acd57affd5c8aee</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;THE PETRIES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Petries are a family unit with which Mulanyin has a significant personal connection, likely linked to her cultural heritage and the colonial system."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71e7bc3cc42173f46acd57affd5c8aee</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LOGAN&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Logan is the place where the story's characters, including Mulanyin, have their upbringing, implicating it in the colonial context of the narrative."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71e7bc3cc42173f46acd57affd5c8aee</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;THE BRISBANE COURIER&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Brisbane Courier is an entity that reported on the public hanging of Dundalli, reflecting its historical role in the colonial media landscape."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71e7bc3cc42173f46acd57affd5c8aee</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;DUNDALLI&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Dundalli is an Aboriginal warrior and resistance leader whose public hanging is a significant historical event, representing colonial violence and the resilience of Aboriginal people."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71e7bc3cc42173f46acd57affd5c8aee</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LOGAN'S OUTSKIRTS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Logan's outskirts epitomizes the marginal spaces where the characters' Indigenous heritage and colonial influences intersect."&lt;SEP&gt;"Logan's outskirts represents the peripheral area where Mulanyin's family resided, implying a rural setting that contrasts with the central colonial society."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71e7bc3cc42173f46acd57affd5c8aee</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MULANYIN'S BROTHERS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Mulanyin's six older brothers are part of her familial support system, sharing similar backgrounds and experiences."&lt;SEP&gt;"The brothers' presence in Mulanyin's life signifies the importance of kinship and shared cultural heritage."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71e7bc3cc42173f46acd57affd5c8aee</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CECILE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Cecile's multiple responsibilities and minimal schooling shed light on the broader social dynamics affecting Indigenous communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cecile, Mulanyin's mother, represents the matriarchal figure in the family, working hard and possibly bridging Indigenous and non-Indigenous worlds."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71e7bc3cc42173f46acd57affd5c8aee</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;WALLY LUCAS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Wally Lucas' Ukrainian background among the Indigenous community adds a layer of cultural complexity to the family's story."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wally Lucas, Melissa Lucashenko's Ukrainian father, reflects the blending of cultures and historical immigration within the family narrative."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71e7bc3cc42173f46acd57affd5c8aee</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MEATWORKER AND CANE CUTTER&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"OCCUPATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Meatworker and cane cutter are the occupations of Mulanyin's father, illustrating the labor-intensive work and economic conditions of the period."&lt;SEP&gt;"These occupations highlight the economic aspects of Indigenous labor under colonial rule."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71e7bc3cc42173f46acd57affd5c8aee</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FISH AND CHIP SHOP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"BUSINESS"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The fish and chip shop represents both a place of commerce and potential cultural exchange for Indigenous women."&lt;SEP&gt;"The fish and chip shop where Cecile worked indicates a common 19th-century Australian business and the mobility of Indigenous women in such jobs.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71e7bc3cc42173f46acd57affd5c8aee</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;DOMESTIC WORKER&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"OCCUPATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Being a domestic worker was a common pathway for Indigenous women, often requiring their movement between different households."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cecile's role as a domestic worker shows the types of work available to women in that era and possibly their own migration between jobs."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71e7bc3cc42173f46acd57affd5c8aee</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LOCAL MARKETS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PLACE"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Local markets are not just economic centers but also social spaces for the exchange of cultural values and community bonds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Local markets where Cecile sold plants are hubs of community and potential economic activity, central to the narrative's setting.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71e7bc3cc42173f46acd57affd5c8aee</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INDIGENOUS NATIVES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Indigenous natives are central to the narrative, representing identity, history, and the societal perceptions that have evolved over time.&lt;SEP&gt;"Indigenous natives refer to the original inhabitants of Australia, whose treatment and portrayal are crucial to the story's examination of identity and history."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71e7bc3cc42173f46acd57affd5c8aee</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;RESISTANCE LEADER&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Dundalli as a resistance leader embodies the struggle for justice and the fight to preserve Indigenous culture."&lt;SEP&gt;"Dundalli as a resistance leader symbolizes the struggle against colonial forces and the fight for Aboriginal rights."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71e7bc3cc42173f46acd57affd5c8aee</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PUBLIC HANGING&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The public hanging of Dundalli is a significant historical event depicting colonial cruelty and its impact on Aboriginal communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"The public hanging of Dundalli is a vivid historical event symbolizing the oppressive nature of colonial law and its consequences for Aboriginal people."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71e7bc3cc42173f46acd57affd5c8aee</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INCORRIGIBLE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Incorrigible, as used to describe Dundalli, reflects the dehumanizing labels used by the colonial system to justify violence against Aboriginal people.&lt;SEP&gt;"The label 'incorrigible' underscores the prejudiced legal system and its dehumanizing effects on Aboriginal individuals."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71e7bc3cc42173f46acd57affd5c8aee</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;GALLOWS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"STRUCTURE"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Gallows are a literal and figurative representation of the colonial system's punitive measures against Indigenous people."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gallows where Dundalli was executed serve as a stark symbol of colonial justice and its harshness.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71e7bc3cc42173f46acd57affd5c8aee</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HANGMAN&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The hangman as the executor of Dundalli's fate is a personification of the enforcement of colonial brutality."&lt;SEP&gt;"The hangman, responsible for Dundalli's execution, exemplifies the role of enforcers of colonial law and order.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71e7bc3cc42173f46acd57affd5c8aee</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;DAWN OF A NEW ERA&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The notion of a new era suggests a future where Indigenous identities and struggles might find different recognition and respect."&lt;SEP&gt;"The suggestion of a new era reflects the hope and potential for change regarding Indigenous rights and identity in the face of ongoing challenges.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71e7bc3cc42173f46acd57affd5c8aee</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LUCASHENKO&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Melissa Lucashenko is an Indigenous Australian writer who has explored her family's history and cultural identity in her work."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e502c0960f2b79b9e3034cfab14d7d34</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CECILE LUCASHENKO&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Cecile Lucashenko is Melissa Lucashenko's mother, who left school early and was determined for her daughter to have more opportunities than she had."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e502c0960f2b79b9e3034cfab14d7d34</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MELISSA LUCASHENKO'S FATHER&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Melissa's father had an uncertain education, having spent time in an orphanage, and later worked in various jobs."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e502c0960f2b79b9e3034cfab14d7d34</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ELEANOR&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GRANDMOTHER"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Eleanor is Melissa Lucashenko's great-grandmother who fought against authorities to avoid being removed as a child and worked as a domestic servant from a young age, facing significant hardships and racial injustice."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e502c0960f2b79b9e3034cfab14d7d34</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CHRISTINA COPSON&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GREAT-GRANDMOTHER"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Christina Copson is Melissa Lucashenko's great-grandmother who faced racial injustice as a young domestic servant and was involved in a self-defence case against an attempted rape by an Aboriginal assailant."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e502c0960f2b79b9e3034cfab14d7d34</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MELISSA LUCASHENKO'S SISTER&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"SISTER"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Melissa's sister moved back to Logan City with her and her family amid mental health difficulties.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e502c0960f2b79b9e3034cfab14d7d34</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;NSW&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"NSW is the Northern Coastal area of New South Wales, where Melissa Lucashenko and her family bought and lived on ancestral land."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e502c0960f2b79b9e3034cfab14d7d34</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;BUNDJALUNG&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ANCESTRAL LAND"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Bundjalung is the ancestral land of the Lucashenko family, located in the coastal area of New South Wales."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e502c0960f2b79b9e3034cfab14d7d34</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;GRIFFITH UNIVERSITY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Griffith University is an educational institution where Melissa Lucashenko graduated with an honours degree in public policy."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e502c0960f2b79b9e3034cfab14d7d34</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;STEAM PIGS&quot;">
