  <data key="d1">"George A. Romero is a filmmaker who revolutionized the zombie genre with his debut feature film, Night of the Living Dead. His work is celebrated for its social commentary and innovative use of practical effects."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d028f70e89e19f331a24fd76e17b9358</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;DUANE JONES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Duane Jones portrayed Ben, a pivotal character in Night of the Living Dead, known for his level-headedness amidst harrowing situations and moral choices during a zombie apocalypse."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d028f70e89e19f331a24fd76e17b9358</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MARILYN EASTMAN&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Marilyn Eastman played Ruth, another resident of the group sheltering in the abandoned house, whose character adds to the complex family dynamics in The Mist."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d028f70e89e19f331a24fd76e17b9358</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HEINZ BENNENT&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Heinz Bennent is not described in detail but is mentioned as a subject of interest, potentially in relation to Andrzej Żuławski's work or film history."&lt;SEP&gt;"Heinz Bennent played Ian Brady, part of a historically infamous couple, in Possession, which explores forbidden love and eternal slumber."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d028f70e89e19f331a24fd76e17b9358&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60a85fa29357fcfe6e32fb5b02b357c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ANDRZEJ ŻUŁAWSKI&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Andrzej Żuławski is a film director known for his transgressive arthouse cinema, whose career was affected by Communist authorities in Poland, and whose cult film Possession is highlighted."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-60a85fa29357fcfe6e32fb5b02b357c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;POSSESSION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"FILM"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Possession is a 1981 psychological horror film directed by Andrzej Żuławski, starring Jurassic Park's Sam Neill and Isabelle Adjani, set in Cold War-era West Berlin about a Russian spy and his wife's mysterious divorce, and the discovery of a horrifying secret."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-60a85fa29357fcfe6e32fb5b02b357c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;COLD WAR-ERA WEST BERLIN&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Cold War-era West Berlin is the setting for Żuławski's film Possession, providing historical context to the narrative."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-60a85fa29357fcfe6e32fb5b02b357c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;JURASSIC PARK&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"FILM"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Jurassic Park is referenced as the film where Sam Neill previously played a role, indicating his acting versatility beyond the film Possession."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-60a85fa29357fcfe6e32fb5b02b357c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SPUTNIK&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"FILM"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Sputnik is a 2020 Russian sci-fi horror film directed by Egor Abramenko, set during the height of Cold War tensions, featuring a psychiatrist dealing with a cosmonaut who becomes a host to an alien organism."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sputnik is a horror film that was initially scheduled to premiere at the Tribeca Film Festival but faced a change in distribution due to the pandemic."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-508b703dace19f04ad098710b945b2ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60a85fa29357fcfe6e32fb5b02b357c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;EGOR ABRAMENKO&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Egor Abramenko is a director, known for his 2020 Russian sci-fi horror film Sputnik, which explores themes of duty and emotional conflict during Cold War tensions."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-60a85fa29357fcfe6e32fb5b02b357c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;OKSANA AKINSHINA&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Oksana Akinshina played the character Tatyana in Abramenko's film Sputnik, depicting a complex character.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-60a85fa29357fcfe6e32fb5b02b357c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FEDOR BONDARCHUK&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Fedor Bondarchuk played the character Konstantin in Abramenko's film Sputnik, portraying a sympathetically complex character.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-60a85fa29357fcfe6e32fb5b02b357c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PYOTR FYODOROV&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Pyotr Fyodorov portrayed the character of a wounded cosmonaut, Konstantin, in Sputnik, contributing to the film's tension and emotional depth."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-60a85fa29357fcfe6e32fb5b02b357c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TATYANA&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CHARACTER"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Tatyana is a character in Abramenko's film Sputnik, portrayed by Oksana Akinshina, a psychiatrist facing a moral and existential dilemma."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-60a85fa29357fcfe6e32fb5b02b357c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;KONSTANTIN&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CHARACTER"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Konstantin is a character in Abramenko's film Sputnik, portrayed by Pyotr Fyodorov, a Russian cosmonaut with an alien organism in his body.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-60a85fa29357fcfe6e32fb5b02b357c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;COLD WAR TENSIONS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Cold War tensions are the historical backdrop for both Żuławski's film Possession and Abramenko's film Sputnik, influencing the narrative and thematic content of these works.")</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-60a85fa29357fcfe6e32fb5b02b357c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SOVIET MILITARY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Soviet military is mentioned as part of the setting in Abramenko's film Sputnik, representing a regulatory body that recruits Tatyana, a psychiatrist, further establishing the political and institutional backdrop of the story."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-60a85fa29357fcfe6e32fb5b02b357c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;COSMONAUT&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PROFESSION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A cosmonaut, specifically Konstantin in Abramenko's film Sputnik, is a key character whose experience brings attention to the physical and psychological impacts of space travel and the encounter with the alien organism."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-60a85fa29357fcfe6e32fb5b02b357c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ALIEN ORGANISM&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The alien organism, living within Konstantin in Sputnik, is the central scientific and existential mystery, driving the narrative and ethical conflicts of the film."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-60a85fa29357fcfe6e32fb5b02b357c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ETHICAL CONFLICT&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"THEME"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Ethical conflict is a recurring theme in both films, explored through the characters' interactions with authority, personal dilemmas, and the larger implications of their actions on humanity."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-60a85fa29357fcfe6e32fb5b02b357c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MORAL AMBIGUITY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"THEME"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Moral ambiguity is present in both Żuławski's Possession and Abramenko's Sputnik, as characters navigate complex situations with overlapping personal and societal responsibilities."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-60a85fa29357fcfe6e32fb5b02b357c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CINEMATOGRAPHY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PRODUCTION ELEMENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Cinematography is noted for its contribution to the atmosphere and storytelling effectiveness in Sputnik, enhancing the film's visual impact and emotional resonance."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-60a85fa29357fcfe6e32fb5b02b357c9</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FILM HISTORY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
